   - "The director of the film '<Film Title>,' <Director Name>, was born in <Place>."  
   - For multiple directors: "The directors of the film '<Film Title>,' <Director 1> and <Director 2>, were born in <Place 1> and <Place 2>, respectively."  
6. Avoid any speculation or inference based on incomplete facts. Do not include unrelated persons or contradictory data. Only use explicitly stated facts from the paragraphs.  
7. The final summary must be fully understandable without requiring reference to any provided paragraphs.

Additional domain-specific considerations:  
- Film titles may include release years or disambiguation; treat them as equivalent to the simpler title form when matching.  
- Director names and their biographical data may appear in separate paragraphs, so cross-reference carefully.  
- If multiple directors share the same film, and only some birthplaces are given, report only those confirmed, omitting others.  
- Avoid conflating individuals with similar names who are unrelated to the film or are mentioned in other contexts.  
- Birthplaces may reflect historical or geographical terms that might be unfamiliar today; use the terminology as stated in the text.

Reasoning approach to solve the task:  
- First, locate the paragraph(s) mentioning the film and its director(s).  
- Then find paragraph(s) that contain birth details for those director(s).  
- Extract the director’s full name(s) and their confirmed place(s) of birth.  
- Finally, formulate a precise, direct summary sentence answering the question about birthplace.

This approach ensures answers are factually accurate, concise, directly relevant to the film asked about, and presented as a clear standalone statement.
